<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE HermitCrabInput SYSTEM "HermitCrabInput.dtd">
<HermitCrabInput>
  <Language>
    <Name>BistratalOverlappingSegmentRepresentation</Name>
    <!-- Synthetic construct-shaped mimic (docs/conformance/representative-typology-basis.md
         S1.2.5 -- "Multi-table: shared-representation-across-tables configuration"). Invented
         CVC(V) roots only, no natural-language lexemes. Representative typological pattern: two
         coexisting phonological/orthographic subsystems within one language that legitimately
         share a surface grapheme/segment representation while belonging to distinct rule sets --
         the general phenomenon Lexical/Stratal Phonology models as separate "strata" (native vs.
         borrowed vocabulary, or stem-level vs. word-level phonology) that can differ in their own
         phonological/morphological rule sets while drawing on an overlapping symbol inventory
         (Kiparsky, "Stratal OT: A synopsis and FAQs"; loanword-phonology native/loan-stratum
         overlap literature; see the research doc's own citations). English's own native/Latinate
         stress-and-affixation split (Kiparsky 1982) is the most commonly cited example of two
         strata sharing most of one alphabet while differing in which rules apply.

         Structural shape: Grammar::char_tables.len() == 2 ("Inner"/table t1, "Outer"/table t2),
         each stratum's own StratumDef::table pointing at a DIFFERENT CharacterDefinitionTable --
         but the two tables are NOT pairwise-disjoint: the literal spelling "s" is a legitimate
         SegmentDefinition in BOTH tables (t1's "cs1", t2's "cs2"), denoting a DIFFERENT segment
         identity in each -- mirroring how a native vs. borrowed stratum can assign the same
         spelled sequence a different phonotactic identity, while sharing most of the rest of the
         alphabet. Each stratum hosts its OWN, independent root lexicon (no rule threads material
         from one stratum's table into the other's) -- deliberately the narrowest grammar that
         still exercises Grammar::char_tables.len() > 1 with an overlapping representation, so this
         fixture's ground truth is a plain, uncomplicated per-stratum lexical lookup, never
         confounded by a separate cross-stratum-rule question.

         Per pg-foma::capability's own multi_table_detail (rust/crates/pg-foma/src/capability.rs),
         this shared representation makes representations_pairwise_disjoint == false, and
         MultiTableFaithfulThreadingPredicate Refuses it (see the paired rust/crates/pg-foma/tests/
         cover_bistratal_overlapping_segment_representation.rs regression test) -- this fixture's
         job is to pin that REFUSAL honestly, plus the oracle's (pg_parse::Morpher, which resolves
         every rule's table via an explicit TableId with no PUA-token collapsing at all) own
         correct, unaffected analysis of every word below. -->
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="t1">
      <Name>Inner</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="ca"><Representations><Representation>a</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cb"><Representations><Representation>b</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="ci"><Representations><Representation>i</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cs1"><Representations><Representation>s</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="t2">
      <Name>Outer</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cd"><Representations><Representation>d</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="ce"><Representations><Representation>e</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cs2"><Representations><Representation>s</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <Strata>
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="t1">
        <Name>Inner</Name>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <LexicalEntry id="eRoot1">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aRoot1"><PhoneticShape>basi</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>ROOT1</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>root1</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <LexicalEntry id="eRoot2">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aRoot2"><PhoneticShape>abis</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>ROOT2</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>root2</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="t2">
        <Name>Outer</Name>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <LexicalEntry id="eRoot3">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aRoot3"><PhoneticShape>des</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>ROOT3</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>root3</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <LexicalEntry id="eRoot4">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aRoot4"><PhoneticShape>sed</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>ROOT4</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>root4</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
    </Strata>
  </Language>
</HermitCrabInput>
